# Read a Source

Input is a path in `~/Research/sources/` or a URL. Output is `~/Research/notes/<slug>.md` and one
updated line in `INDEX.md`. The note is what future sessions will trust, so its quotes matter more
than its prose.

## Get the text

For a local PDF, fetch it through the harness's web tool with the local path — it extracts the text
without any extra installation:

```
web_access  op=fetch  input=~/Research/sources/<file>.pdf
```

For a URL, fetch the page the same way. Then check the extraction: if it yields under ~500 words for a
substantial document, the extraction failed — report that and do not write a note from a fragment.
Scanned PDFs have no text layer; say so, and note that OCR is needed rather than guessing content.

## Write the note

Fixed sections, in this order. Short and factual beats long and fluent.

```markdown
---
source: <file or url>
authors: <surnames + et al.>
year: <year>
title: <full title>
venue: <journal or preprint server>
doi_or_url: <identifier>
study_type: <RCT | cohort | cross-sectional | qualitative | review | documentation | preprint>
tier: <A | B | C | D>
read_on: <YYYY-MM-DD>
---

# <Short title>

## Claims
- <One checkable claim, stated plainly>. "<verbatim quote>" (p. N)
- ...

## Method and sample
- Design, N, population, duration, what was compared. Every number exactly as printed.

## Limits the authors state
- ...

## What this does not support
- <The tempting generalisation this source cannot carry, and why>
- <Population, setting, or duration that would be needed for it to carry>

## Contradicts
- <Note file and claim, or "none found in ~/Research/notes/">.
```

Rules for `Claims`:

- **No quote, no claim.** Every bullet carries a verbatim quote and a locator (page, section, or table).
  If a sentence cannot be anchored, write `(unverified)` after it or delete it.
- Preserve numbers exactly — sample sizes, effect sizes, confidence intervals, p-values, dates, doses.
  Never round, never re-derive, never "approximately".
- One claim per bullet. A bullet containing "and" usually hides two claims, one of which is unsupported.
- Do not put your interpretation in `Claims`. Interpretation belongs in `What this does not support`.
- If the abstract and the results disagree, the results win and the disagreement is worth one line.

## Update the index

Replace that source's `not read yet` line in `INDEX.md` with the two or three claims a future session
would need to decide whether to reopen the note:

```markdown
- <slug> | <year> | tier B | plans the deficit, not plan recall (45 vs 45, p <.001)
```

## Before finishing

- If the note contradicts an existing note, say so to the user directly and cite both files. Do not
  silently overwrite the older note.
- If the source is weaker than its abstract suggests (small N, self-report only, author self-evaluation),
  state that in one sentence at the top of the reply, before the summary.
- Do not edit, rename, or delete anything in `sources/`.
